# /etc/infra-cod/database.env - the connection target shared by every DB client.
#
# Installed as root:root 0640. It contains NO secret: production reaches
# PostgreSQL by peer authentication over the Unix socket, where the OS user and
# the database role are mapped 1:1 in pg_ident.conf. There is no `DATABASE_URL`
# and no `PGPASSWORD` anywhere in production; a password-bearing URL would be a
# second, weaker authentication path beside the peer mapping.
#
# What is deliberately NOT here:
#
#   PGUSER   Every unit sets its own role, because web, worker, backup, migrator
#            and health are different roles. A shared PGUSER would make the
#            mapping decorative: one compromised process could name another's
#            role and nothing would notice. See deploy/systemd/README.md for the
#            full matrix.
#
#   PGHOST   It IS here, and it is explicit because every unit sets
#            PrivateTmp=true. A private /tmp means the default socket path
#            (/tmp/.s.PGSQL.5432) does not exist inside the unit, so a fallback
#            would fail with a confusing "no such file" instead of connecting.
#
# This file is the contract Stage 2's setup-postgresql-production.sh must satisfy:
# the server has to listen on the socket at PGHOST, and pg_hba.conf must contain
# `local all all peer map=infra_cod_map` with the mappings listed in
# deploy/systemd/README.md.

PGHOST=/var/run/postgresql
PGDATABASE=infra_cod
